### A12. Admin

Access: /admin route. Sign-in with Firebase Auth email and password. Users must also enter a passcode for a second factor, verified server-side by /api/admin/passcode (constant-time comparison against a salted hash stored in environment or Firestore admin-only doc). Never check passcodes in the browser.

Authorization: Firestore security rules and Storage rules check request.auth.token.role. Role is assigned by an owner-only server function (/api/admin/grant) using the Admin SDK. Clients cannot self-assign roles.

Two modules, controlled by settings.site.tier:

Assets module (available to role "assets" and "owner"):
- Products: create, edit, duplicate, archive, publish. Image upload to Storage with client-side resize to max 2000px and WebP conversion, plus server rule enforcing content type image/* and size under 8 MB.
- Specs: entered against the category's spec schema. Required fields enforced by Zod. Units shown in the UI.
- Categories: order, visibility, hero image.
- Discounts: create codes and automatic discounts, set scope, dates, minimums, caps.
- Orders: view, filter by channel and status, mark WhatsApp orders confirmed or cancelled, add notes.
- Bulk CSV import for products with a downloadable template and row-level validation errors.

CMS module (available only when tier is "cms" and role is "cms" or "owner"):
- Theme editor: color tokens with hex input and live preview, typography pairing from an approved list, container width, radius, spacing scale, motion level, grain and blur intensity. Saves to settings/theme with a version bump. The app listens to the theme doc and applies CSS variables live.
- Copy editor: every text block on the homepage and segment pages is registered in a content map with a key, default value, and optional override. Editors change overrides, never code.
- Section manager: order and visibility of homepage sections (not arbitrary layout building, which is out of scope for the middle tier).
- Policies editor: rich text with sanitization.
- Announcement bar: text, link, enabled, schedule.

Tier rules:
- Tier "assets": CMS routes return 404 and the CMS nav item is hidden. Theme tokens still apply from the default theme file, not editable.
- Tier "cms": full access. Upgrade from assets to cms is a single settings change by the owner, with no redeploy.
- Middle tier (recommended for negotiation): assets plus theme tokens only (colors, type pairing, width, radius, motion level), with copy and section manager disabled. Implement by feature flags: theme.enabled, copy.enabled, sections.enabled.

### A13. Security requirements (non-negotiable)

- No secret keys in the client bundle. Only VITE_ prefixed public config.
- Firestore rules: products, categories, settings, and discounts are readable publicly where published or public; writable only by admins with the correct role. orders are not publicly readable; a reference lookup goes through /api/order/lookup which requires the reference plus the phone number used at checkout.
- Storage rules: public read for product images; write restricted to admins; content type and size validation.
- Rate limiting on /api/price, /api/order/create, and /api/order/lookup (per IP, via Vercel KV or an in-memory fallback in dev).
- Input validation with Zod on every API route. Reject unknown fields.
- Paystack webhook signature verification is mandatory. Idempotency keyed on Paystack reference.
- Sanitize all rich text (DOMPurify on the server side at save time, and escape on render).
- CSP headers in vercel.json. Restrict script sources to self and the Firebase, Paystack, and Google Fonts hosts.
- Audit log: every admin write records actor uid, role, timestamp, and before/after hash in an auditLog collection (write-only from server).
- Never log customer phone or email in server logs.

### A14. Performance and quality budgets

- LCP under 2.5 s on mid-range Android over 4G (Lighthouse mobile). Hero image preloaded, fonts with font-display swap and preconnect.
- CLS under 0.1. Reserve image aspect ratios.
- JS for the homepage under 200 KB gzipped. Route-level code splitting; GSAP loaded per route that uses it.
- Accessibility: WCAG 2.2 AA. Visible focus states, keyboard support for the cart drawer (focus trap, Escape to close), alt text required on product images, color contrast checked for muted text (#9AA3B8 on #05070D passes AA for normal text).
- Reduced motion respected everywhere.
- SEO: unique title and description per page, Product JSON-LD on product pages, LocalBusiness JSON-LD on the homepage (name, address Ikeja Lagos, telephone, email), sitemap.xml and robots.txt generated at build.

### A15. Working rules for the AI builder

1. Build one phase at a time. Do not generate future phases early.
2. Before writing code for a phase, list the files you will create or change and the acceptance checks.
3. Keep all money logic in lib/money.ts and lib/pricing.ts with unit tests. Keep UI free of arithmetic.
4. Never hardcode business copy, prices, or contact details in components. Read from settings or the content map.
5. Every API route validates input with Zod, returns typed errors, and is covered by at least one test.
6. When a requirement is ambiguous, implement the safer option and add a TODO with the question in a single file, DECISIONS.md.
7. Produce working code that runs with npm run dev and npm run build with zero type errors before ending a phase.
8. Summarize at the end of each phase: what changed, what passes, what is still a placeholder.

